'' A WIDE RIGHT SIDE ASSIGNED TO A UDT WHOSE 'operator let' TAKES A STRING is
'' converted to narrow and passed, not declined. 'd = left( w, 2 )' over
'' 'operator let( byref s as string )' left d UNCHANGED and reported "1 statements
'' not lowered" -- AfxNova's DWSTRING path-trimming helpers write exactly this.
''
'' The shape below is fbc's, measured: take the wide LEFT, convert the result into a
'' narrow temporary, call the operator with THAT, and delete the temporary. The
'' narrow right side one line down ('d = left( s, 1 )') always worked and is here so
'' that a fix which only ever chooses the wide path cannot pass.
''
'' Runtime output is differenced against fbc by build/goldenrun.sh, which is what
'' proves the conversion is the right way round: both print
'' "let wide: x y" then "let narrow: p".
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/udt-let-wide-rvalue.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered

'' The wide LEFT, then the wide-to-narrow conversion, then the operator.
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_WstrLeft
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_WstrAssignToA
'' EXPECT-OUT: call void _ZN2AX3DWSaSER8FBSTRING

'' The narrow one goes straight in -- no conversion call in front of it.
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_LEFT
